  "content": {
    "title": "Recap of the Thursday SpaceX rocket launch from Florida",
    "text": "10:28 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX booster landsBrooke EdwardsThe Falcon 9 first stage booster just landed aboard SpaceX's drone ship Just Read the Instructions on the Atlantic Ocean, completing its 27th mission.10:20 am ET March 19, 2026Liftoff!Brooke EdwardsSpaceX has just launched the Falcon 9 rocket carrying 29 Starlink satellites from Cape Canaveral SLC-40!10:13 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch webcast beginsBrooke EdwardsSpaceX's launch webcast hosted on X (formerly Twitter) is now posted above, right below the countdown clock.10:10 am ET March 19, 2026T-10 minutes until SpaceX launch from FloridaBrooke EdwardsSkies over Brevard County are cloudy, possibly making seeing this rocket for long difficult. Be sure to look early to ensure you do not miss it.10:03 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch in Florida todayBrooke EdwardsToday's SpaceX booster also launched the CRS-28 resupply mission. That launch occurred on June 5, 2023, and sent a Cargo Dragon spacecraft to the ISS.9:49 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX fueling ahead of launch todayBrooke EdwardsVisual cues indicate Falcon 9 fueling procedures are now underway at Launch Complex 40, though SpaceX has yet to make an official announcement.That means today's Starlink mission is now locked in to lift off at 10:20 a.m. without any countdown delays, or else the launch must be postponed.9:43 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch prep underwayBrooke EdwardsBrevard County Emergency Management officials have activated the agency's launch operations support team ahead of SpaceX’s upcoming Falcon 9 launch.9:33 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX rocket launch from FloridaBrooke EdwardsToday’s Falcon 9 will land on the Just Read the Instructionsdrone ship in the Atlantic Ocean. The only sound anticipated will be the rocket’s familiar rumble.The Falcon 9 will be ferried back to port Canaveral, where it can be seen during a short stopping period.9:24 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch from Cape CanaveralBrooke EdwardsHere's a flashback photo of today's booster. The same booster launching today on its 27th flight launched NASA's SpaceX Crew-5 back on Oct. 22, 2022 to the International Space Station.9:15 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch in FloridaBrooke EdwardsSpaceX has updated its countdown clock, and is still targeting 10:20 a.m. for this morning's liftoff.Clouds are starting to clear in spots along the Space Coast.6:23 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch delayedBrooke EdwardsSpaceX has pushed the launch back until no earlier than 10:20 a.m. FLORIDA TODAY will return with live updates one hour prior to liftoff.5:59 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch holdingBrooke EdwardsWhat could have been a 'jellyfish' launch is being delayed. SpaceX will attempt the launch later in the morning. FLORIDA TODAY will update with a new T-0 when SpaceX updates.SpaceX has until 10:35 a.m. to make the liftoff.5:50 am ET March 19, 2026SpaceX launch countdown timelineBrooke Edwards If SpaceX is still targeting 6:35 a.m., fueling should begin shortly. Here's a behind-the-scenes rundown of SpaceX’s countdown timeline. T-minus:38 minutes: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for propellant load.35 minutes: Rocket-grade kerosene and first-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.16 minutes: Second-stage liquid oxygen loading begins.7 minutes: Falcon 9 begins engine chill prior to launch.1 minute: Command flight computer begins final prelaunch checks; propellant tank pressurization to flight pressure begins.45 seconds: SpaceX launch director verifies “go” for launch.3 seconds: Engine controller commands engine ignition sequence to start.0 seconds: Liftoff.5:35 am ET March 19, 2026T-1 hour until SpaceX launch from Cape CanaveralBrooke EdwardsLiftoff is still set for 6:35 a.m., meaning a high chance of a 'jellyfish' over Florida and beyond. The sun, just below the horizon, will likely illuminate the rocket's trail.Sunrise in Cape Canaveral comes at 7:28 a.m. today.5:22 am ET March 19, 2026Today's launch from Florida sees well-flown boosterBrooke EdwardsToday's SpaceX booster is flying for the 27th time. Previous missions include:NASA's Crew-5GPS III Space Vehicle 06Inmarsat I6-F2CRS-28 (ISS resupply mission)Intelsat G-37NG-20 (ISS resupply mission)TD719 previous Starlink missions5:02 am ET March 19, 2026Possible 'jellyfish' SpaceX launch from FloridaBrooke EdwardsIf this launch goes at 6:35 a.m. and weather conditions are just right, it's highly possible the Space Coast − and those up the east coast − will see a colorful 'jellyfish' effect.",
